{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a non-inductive 'conventionalist decision' to act as if the best-tested theory is true, accepting the cost that this preference offers no guarantee of future success or increased verisimilitude.","counterconditions":["If the preference implies a belief that the theory is more likely to be true.","If the decision relies on any assumption of nature's uniformity beyond the logical possibility of falsification.","If the 'cost' is denied, specifically the lack of probabilistic justification for future performance."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Methodological Conventionalism: The scientist adopts a rule of action (prefer the best-tested) not because it predicts truth, but because it is the most rational available strategy for error elimination, distinct from an inductive belief that the future will resemble the past.","optional_refs":["51ef8f97a9c0ad0ac451cd6c221e1e92ab89fa2614971e29531d63b83d1904ca","af00a557d9cfb86db6668e7e9782bc3c03c9d48dfbd42bc8bf72bf339f03defb"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference smuggles in induction because selecting a theory for future action based on past test survival logically presupposes that past performance is a reliable guide to future reliability, which is the definition of the inductive principle Popper sought to eliminate.","counterconditions":["If the preference can be shown to rely purely on the logical status of currently unfalsified theories without reference to future expectations.","If 'preference' is redefined as mere 'temporary retention' without any implication of superior fitness for future tasks.","If the cost of abandoning the preference is shown to be total scientific paralysis, forcing a pragmatic rather than logical justification."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Hidden Inductive Leap: The act of 'preferring' implies an expectation of future utility or truth-likeness derived from past data, which cannot be justified deductively and thus re-introduces the very problem Popper claimed to solve.","optional_refs":["0e7c744798ee87399a050abd6cd2e04b6bc10eca0bf07854be5e563dd7989460","605ff1954b616ea5b2ed456495e8952a3412cf9422e614619b532c73a5f4144d"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is defensible via an evolutionary analogy where theories compete in an ecosystem of tests; the 'fittest' (best-corroborated) is selected not because it is 'true' or 'probable', but because it is the only surviving variant, making the preference a description of survival rather than a prediction of future dominance.","counterconditions":["If the analogy breaks down because biological fitness is retrospective while scientific preference is prospective.","If the 'selection' of a theory for application implies a belief in its continued fitness.","If the extinction of all current theories leaves no basis for preference, revealing the emptiness of the survival metric."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Evolutionary Epistemology: Scientific progress mirrors natural selection. Corroboration is a report of past survival, not a prophecy. Preferring the survivor is rational because it is the only one left standing, not because we expect it to survive tomorrow.","optional_refs":["0e7c744798ee87399a050abd6cd2e04b6bc10eca0bf07854be5e563dd7989460","f759e1797c3c7358c9f05eca44bdc692d699cccb94f075e8e216f35bb555d77f"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The dilemma is false because Popper's 'preference' is not epistemic (about truth) but pragmatic (about problem-solving efficiency); we prefer the better-corroborated theory because it has higher content and has survived more attempts to refute it, making it a richer tool for generating new problems, regardless of its truth probability.","counterconditions":["If high content and resilience are themselves indicators of probable truth.","If the goal of science is defined as 'truth' rather than 'knowledge growth', rendering pragmatic utility insufficient.","If a false theory with high content leads science astray more effectively than a boring true one."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Problem-Shift Utility: The value of a theory lies in its ability to generate new, testable predictions (high content). Better-corroborated theories have demonstrated high content and resilience, making them superior engines for driving the growth of knowledge, even if they are false.","optional_refs":["49b933dadc62b63b59098c57873624285a0b24eade8b3a8d1fc6a59fb36c6105","2b2dc8431b18b8aa0541623ac92ad4c34ba8c1437a0d46079e56d4f73cfd40a0"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6}],"simulation_proposals":[]}